<H3>Computer-assisted translation</H3>

 <p>We are heavily committed to the use of <span class="mypopover" data-toggle="modal" data-target="#Modal_Def_CAT" title="">Computer-assisted translation software</span> (CAT) in our translation work.  We have chosen three market-leading CAT systems for our work: SDL Trados, Star Transit and SDL Passolo (though we are always willing to use other tools where we see that the benefits are clear).  Each of these sophisticated systems has its particular advantages, depending on the type of text that needs to be translated.  Experience shows that the use of such technology brings enormous benefits to customers, both in terms of translation quality and in terms of price.</p>

 <p>Using SDL Passolo, for example, you can even integrate your localization process automatically into your development cycle, saving you the cost of manual translation both before and after builds, reducing your turnaround time and increasing your agility.</p>   

<H3>Translation Memory</H3>

<p>One of the major benefits of <span class="mypopover" data-toggle="modal" data-target="#Modal_Def_TM" title="">translation memory</span> (TM) tools is their capacity to recognize texts that we have already translated in the past.  The most obvious advantage of this capacity is that, if you design your workflows right (as we do), no translation need ever be done twice. Apart from the obvious cost savings that TM can provide, it also helps enormously to preserve consistency in the target language. The use of "<span class="mypopover" data-toggle="modal" data-target="#Modal_Def_FuzzyMatch" title="">fuzzy matching</span>" (i.e. the ability of CAT software to identify previous translations similar, but not identical, to the one currently on screen) helps the translator to write comparable sentences in a similar style and with the use of consistent terminology.</p>

<H3>Terminology Management</H3>

<p>To ensure absolute consistency in terminology, the <span class="mypopover" data-toggle="modal" data-target="#Modal_Def_TermMgt" title="">terminology management</span> functions provided by CAT software are absolutely essential.  Ideally, each client's essential terminology should be defined before beginning translation. That's why we often ask  clients to provide lists of key words and expressions &ndash; along with any definitions and preferred translations they may have &ndash; well before any translation begins. Where this is impossible, we create and maintain a customized terminology database in conjunction with our client contacts.</p>

<H3>Alignment</H3>

<p>Where you have texts that have already been translated, send us the source and target texts and we'll use what's generally referred to as "alignment functionality" to record each translation in your customized translation memory.  In cases where the quality of legacy translations is dubious, it is often a good idea to edit translation segments directly inside the translation memory utility to ensure that errors are permanently removed from customer texts &#8211; in both source and target languages.</p>

<H3>Protecting source code and markup &#8211; filtering and marking code</H3>

<p>The technology we use allows us to filter XML-based files to include only nodes that contain translatable text and to label mark-up and scripting code within XML, HTML and other text formats as protected. This ensures that they are not accidentally deleted or modified during translation. Careful use of placeholder and tag definition settings ensures that translators can see all translatable texts in their context, speeding and improving the quality of their work.</p> 

<H3>File types</H3>

<p>Our translation tools allow us to translate files in the widest possible range of formats, including all MS Office, OpenOffice and editable Adobe formats, HTML and XML. We're also able to work directly within Microsoft MFC and .NET resource files, or even directly in compiled programs.  Java resource files (*.jar) are also part of our routine, as are Android-standard key/value files and many others, (including, for example, files containing the results of database queries).</p>

<p>Just let us know within which file type your text needs to be translated.  We translate your translatables, leaving your functional tags and code untouched.</p>

<H3>In the age of big data &#8211; machine translation is not quite there yet.</H3>

<p>There has been a lot of optimistic talk going back as far as the 1960s in the translation and IT industries about the potential of IT in automating the difficult and still time-consuming process of translating texts from one language to any other. However, progress in the area has so far been disappointing. The latest methods in what is now referred to as machine translation are now less centred around very complex rules-based translation systems (which try to analyse the source text according to the grammatical structure of its source language and render it in the target language according to language rules). The "age of big data" ushered in by the capacity of today's technology to crunch enormous amounts of data with astounding speed is now focussing more attention on what are referred to as statistical methods of translating, where translations are offered on the basis of the statistical analysis of previous translations of similar texts.  The most famous (and perhaps most powerful) example of such data-crunching translation systems is <a href="http://translate.google.com/" target="_newtab">Google translate</a>.</p>

<p>While the latest machine translation systems, and most obviously Google Translate, are huge improvements on previous attempts to develop machine translation and can provide decipherable translations in some types of text and for some language pairs, they are still some distance from providing any more than a marginal labour-saving device for providing the quality required for the vast majority of translation tasks.  However, we are keeping a close eye on developments in the machine translation sector, and will consider including the use of such technology in our wider workflows if and when we see it as providing potential benefits for our clients.</p>

